update public.questions set explanation = $e$A packet is a header, then the payload (the data), then a trailer. The trailer carries error-detection data, typically a checksum or CRC, which lets the receiver spot corruption in transit. The sender address and protocol version sit in the header, not the trailer.$e$ where id = '17000fc7-d790-458d-aa32-60ff536d673c';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Starting from rest, v = u + at with u = 0, a = g = 9.81 m s⁻² and t = 3 s, so v = 9.81 × 3 = 29.4 m s⁻¹ downward. The 88.3 m s⁻¹ option multiplies g by 9 (a common squaring error), and 9.81 m s⁻¹ is just g.$e$ where id = '185c1244-872d-4646-a917-0d7c6c2ec4ab';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Power rule: d/dx xⁿ = n xⁿ⁻¹. With n = 4 the derivative is 4x³. The exponent drops by one and the old exponent multiplies the coefficient, so 3x⁴ and 4x⁴ are both incorrect.$e$ where id = '188f91bc-82f0-4740-b586-532881c9f7ca';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$The gradient of y = x² at (2, 4) is dy/dx = 2x = 4. The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so its gradient is the negative reciprocal: −1/4. The 1/4 option is the reciprocal without the sign change.$e$ where id = '19176702-0548-4043-acee-052ac606c4f5';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$The LCD of 3/4 and 2/3 is 12. Rewrite as 9/12 + 8/12 = 17/12. Since 17 > 12, take out one whole: 17/12 = 1 5/12. The 1 ⅙ and 1 ¼ options come from using the wrong denominators.$e$ where id = '19bdbd51-de1e-4a79-8ea7-cf2f76da1dd5';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Constructive interference occurs when the path difference is a whole number of wavelengths, nλ (n = 0, 1, 2, …). Waves then arrive in phase: crests meet crests and troughs meet troughs. Half-integer multiples, (n + ½)λ, give destructive interference.$e$ where id = '1b458c9c-4edd-4a6d-860a-f99d1b2cce9c';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Constant velocity means acceleration is zero. By Newton's second law the resultant force is F = ma = 0. The individual forces (weight, friction, etc.) may be non-zero, but they must balance so that they sum to zero.$e$ where id = '1c38339c-9b67-4abb-9e7e-37ef902d3d1c';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Kepler's third law: T² ∝ r³. A larger orbital radius therefore gives a longer period. For example, doubling r increases T by a factor of 2√2 ≈ 2.83.$e$ where id = '1e4b0dfa-eb51-4933-b9bc-ab9c7a4111a5';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$In a star topology every device connects to one central switch or hub. If that central device fails, all communication stops, so it is a single point of failure. Speed and addressing are not the defining weakness of this design.$e$ where id = '1ea9bfbb-c8b0-4db5-ab57-501e6e7df6a1';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$16^(3/4) = (⁴√16)³ = 2³ = 8. Read the denominator as the root and the numerator as the power. Check with the exponent rule: 16^(3/4) = (16^(1/4))³ = 2³ = 8.$e$ where id = '1ec75882-8784-492e-899f-d2fbda6b70cd';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$For an AP, uₙ = a + (n − 1)d with a = 3 and d = 7 − 3 = 4. So u₁₅ = 3 + 14 × 4 = 3 + 56 = 59. Check the 15th term count: there are 14 common differences between term 1 and term 15.$e$ where id = '1f4acc37-8281-444d-96f0-eeda198522c9';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Unauthorised access to a computer system is a criminal offence under computer misuse law, such as the UK Computer Misuse Act 1990. Intent to cause harm is not required for the access itself, and resigning afterwards does not make it lawful.$e$ where id = '1f5bd7b8-e054-4b0c-9042-49f068fc71a3';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Volume of a cylinder: V = πr²h. Substituting r = 3 and h = 10 gives π × 9 × 10 = 90π cm³. The 270π option uses r³ instead of r², and 30π drops a factor of 3.$e$ where id = '216d23f3-cd02-485b-81a2-ab3e514c4cf4';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Kinetic energy: Eₖ = ½mv². With m = 2 kg and v = 3 m s⁻¹, Eₖ = ½ × 2 × 9 = 9 J. Kinetic energy depends on the square of the speed, so 3 J (which uses v, not v²) is wrong.$e$ where id = '22a84688-ed59-4522-aa9a-c0dbe8b44151';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$The interquartile range is the spread of the middle half of the data: IQR = Q₃ − Q₁ = 20 − 12 = 8. It is a difference, not a sum or a product, which is why 32 and 16 are wrong.$e$ where id = '24b83216-6c2e-495f-87d5-f7c54e42690f';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Bearings are measured clockwise from North. East is a quarter turn clockwise from North, so its bearing is 090°. South is 180° and west is 270°, so 270° describes the west road, not the east one.$e$ where id = '2712e091-07fb-4a6a-b141-d67bf2f85102';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$In peer-to-peer networks every node can act as both client and server, and resources are shared roughly equally. Client–server networks, by contrast, rely on a dedicated server with central administration.$e$ where id = '283e9f88-d6d8-4d9e-9291-b3e93132d8e2';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$To integrate xⁿ, add 1 to the power and divide by the new power: ∫x³ dx = x⁴/4 + c. Differentiating x⁴/4 gives x³, which checks the answer. The 3x² and 4x³ options are derivatives, not integrals.$e$ where id = '288186e7-22ca-4394-881d-5d28adcd72ae';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$For a Poisson distribution, P(X = k) = e^(−λ) λ^k / k!. With λ = 2 and k = 0: P(X = 0) = e^(−2) × 1 / 0! = e^(−2) ≈ 0.135. The 1 − e⁻² option is P(X ≥ 1).$e$ where id = '28c8e442-c48b-42ca-830b-b4f0f407bdd5';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Encapsulation is an OOP principle: data and the methods that operate on it are bundled into one unit (a class), and the internal state is hidden behind a public interface. Recursion, paging and sorting are unrelated techniques.$e$ where id = '28fca24a-ddb8-4cf1-9da3-569a78c9bbdb';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Add in binary column by column from the right. 1011 + 0110: the 1s give 1 + 0 = 1; the 2s give 1 + 1 = 10 (write 0, carry 1); the 4s give 0 + 1 + 1 = 10 (write 0, carry 1); the 8s give 1 + 0 + 1 = 10 (write 0, carry 1), leaving a final 1. Result 10001 = 17.$e$ where id = '2980bb20-06ea-4ca3-99bb-3399b8a981a9';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Insertion sort builds a sorted section one element at a time, shifting larger items to make room. If the data is nearly sorted, very few shifts are needed, so it approaches linear time. On reversed data it is at its worst, O(n²).$e$ where id = '29fb0fb6-e638-461e-b0c4-6d3f1158ebb4';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Bias in AI systems usually comes from the data the model learns from. If the training data under-represents groups or reflects past prejudice, the model reproduces it. The hardware (CPUs, screens) and encryption do not cause bias.$e$ where id = '2b166cd0-04bd-4551-a7da-c59ed80434c3';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Standardise: Z = (8 − 10)/2 = −1, since σ = √4 = 2. Then P(X < 8) = Φ(−1) ≈ 0.1587. The 0.8413 option is Φ(1), the complement, which is P(X > 12) and not what is asked.$e$ where id = '2d26017c-a99c-48eb-bc18-2b2bbb3e43dd';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$1 km = 1000 m, so 2.5 km = 2.5 × 1000 = 2500 m. Converting up a unit means a smaller number, and converting down means a larger one. The 25 000 m option multiplies by 10 000 by mistake.$e$ where id = '2d3e418c-6bd2-4cff-b825-f56c5262d2e6';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Polarisation only applies to transverse waves, where oscillations are perpendicular to the direction of travel. Light is a transverse electromagnetic wave, so it can be polarised. Sound is longitudinal and cannot be polarised.$e$ where id = '2dfd52d8-ef07-49fa-b507-fbd83fb37d46';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Electrical power is P = VI. With V = 10 V and I = 2 A, P = 10 × 2 = 20 W. Check the units: volts × amperes = watts.$e$ where id = '2e88ba2c-6b40-4537-8a8e-3fdd5ce572e3';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$The double-angle formula for tangent is tan 2A = 2 tan A / (1 − tan²A). It is derived from tan(A + B) with B = A. Note the minus sign in the denominator; the plus-sign version belongs to sin/cos forms.$e$ where id = '2f1fc959-43b2-475a-9802-452f797a8756';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$35% means 35/100 = 0.35. Then 0.35 × 240 = £84. As a check, 10% of £240 is £24, so 30% is £72 and 5% is £12, giving £84.$e$ where id = '314da4bf-8e07-462b-8e76-a2f30b0709c2';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Use the linearity of expectation: E(a + bX) = a + bE(X). Here E(5 − 2X) = 5 − 2E(X) = 5 − 2(−2) = 5 + 4 = 9. Note that the sign of E(X) matters when the coefficient is negative.$e$ where id = '336c972f-7fcf-43f3-b152-dfaaa2da104d';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Releasing software that is known to have safety-critical faults without disclosure breaches professional codes of conduct (such as those of the BCS or ACM), which require competence and honesty. Calling it standard practice or always legal misses the point.$e$ where id = '3478258e-0c41-4243-ad51-c4018df1e7c8';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Using the log rule d/dx (ln u) = u′/u, with u = 5x and u′ = 5: d/dx(ln 5x) = 5/(5x) = 1/x. The 5/x option forgets to divide by u, and ln 5 is a constant, so its derivative is zero.$e$ where id = '35962340-d338-4afa-85db-e96361639a15';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$For any events, P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B). Independence gives P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B) = 0.7 × 0.4 = 0.28. So P(A ∪ B) = 0.7 + 0.4 − 0.28 = 0.82. The 1.1 option is what you get by omitting the overlap.$e$ where id = '36aef05d-3939-45d9-a506-658c6da134e0';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Newton's second law: F = ma, so a = F/m = 6 N ÷ 2 kg = 3 m s⁻². The 12 m/s² option multiplies instead of dividing, and 0.33 inverts the ratio.$e$ where id = '36cb7973-d0c9-459f-8d78-e0216c7d063e';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$By the chain rule, d/dx e^(g(x)) = g′(x) e^(g(x)). Here g(x) = 3x and g′(x) = 3, so the derivative is 3e^(3x). The e^(3x)/3 option is an integral, not a derivative.$e$ where id = '3867c540-6cab-43dc-9d66-dd29c0ad22ef';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Area of a circle: A = πr². With π = 22/7 and r = 7 cm, A = (22/7) × 49 = 22 × 7 = 154 cm². The 153.94 cm² option uses π ≈ 3.14159, which is not the value given in the question.$e$ where id = '38d1ac75-e488-4ee3-8c6c-8f3de8f149b1';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$For Poisson X with λ = 3, P(X = 0) = e^(−λ) × λ⁰ / 0! = e^(−3) ≈ 0.0498. Since λ⁰ = 1 and 0! = 1, only the exponential term remains. e³ is much greater than 1, and a probability cannot exceed 1.$e$ where id = '38e4c64d-8a9c-414b-85a2-5d9c3d21dbda';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$i is defined by i² = −1. This is the basis of complex arithmetic: powers of i cycle through i, −1, −i, 1.$e$ where id = '390f5bf6-f653-47be-acfb-f6e00414ee97';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Momentum: p = mv = 0.5 kg × 8 m s⁻¹ = 4 kg m s⁻¹. Momentum is the product of mass and velocity, so the 0.0625 option divides by the wrong quantity and 16 doubles the answer.$e$ where id = '39b318e9-8abb-4938-8360-13a7e44f2f70';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$For X ~ B(n, p), P(X = 0) = (1 − p)ⁿ. Here n = 6 and p = 0.25, so P(X = 0) = 0.75⁶ ≈ 0.178. The 0.0156 option is 0.25⁶, which would be the probability of six successes, the opposite extreme.$e$ where id = '39eb56c2-6660-4e2f-8710-cbe21b065864';
